# Audit RAG — Enterprise Document Intelligence Platform

A FastAPI + SQLAlchemy + Qdrant + Ollama platform for uploading, parsing,
classifying, chunking, embedding, and querying enterprise documents (invoices,
contracts, policies, financial statements, tax documents, etc.), with:

- **Hybrid RAG chat** — ask questions grounded in your document corpus, with
  citations back to source pages.
- **Document-scoped chat** — pick specific document(s) and get answers reasoned
  directly from their full content, not just semantic-search fragments.
- **AI Audit Agent** — a map-reduce LLM pipeline that reviews a batch of
  documents, maintains a running memory of findings across them, and produces
  a structured Markdown/PDF audit report.
- **Deterministic extraction + rule engine** — facts, entities, and line items
  extracted from documents feed a rule engine that flags risk before any LLM
  ever sees the document.

---

## Architecture overview

```
Upload
  │
  ▼
Classification (AI-based via Ollama, or rule-based fallback)
  │
  ▼
Parsing (PyMuPDF digital text + tables → Tesseract OCR → Qwen2.5-VL vision fallback)
  │
  ▼
Canonicalization (unified CanonicalDocument schema)
  │
  ▼
Metadata / Entity / Fact / Line-Item extraction
  │
  ▼
Rule Engine (deterministic risk findings)
  │
  ▼
Chunking (type-routed: invoice/contract/policy/financial-statement/generic)
  │
  ▼
Embedding (Ollama nomic-embed-text, with fallback chain)
  │
  ▼
Qdrant indexing (dense vectors + rich payload for filtered search)
  │
  ▼
Retrieval (hybrid dense + BM25 + RRF fusion) → Reranking (cross-encoder)
  │
  ▼
Chat (grounded prompt, citation extraction, response validation)
```

Two entry points bypass the retrieval/reranking pipeline by design:

- **Document-scoped chat**: when the user explicitly selects one or more
  documents (rather than asking an open-ended question), the assistant loads
  that document's full content directly from the chunk store instead of
  running semantic search against it. This avoids retrieval risk entirely for
  "tell me about X.pdf" style questions.
- **Audit Agent**: runs a map-reduce summarization pass per document
  (batched by token budget), maintains a JSON memory object across documents
  in a run, and synthesizes a final report — independent of the chat
  retrieval path.

---

## Tech stack

| Layer | Choice |
|---|---|
| API | FastAPI, async SQLAlchemy |
| DB | SQLite (dev) via `aiosqlite`; async engine |
| Migrations | Alembic (batch mode enabled for SQLite `ALTER TABLE` support) |
| Vector store | Qdrant |
| Embeddings | Ollama (`nomic-embed-text`), swappable via `EMBEDDING_PROVIDER` |
| LLM | Ollama (`qwen2.5:latest` for chat/classification, `llama3.1` for the audit agent), local or cloud-hosted |
| Reranking | `sentence-transformers` cross-encoder (`ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2`) |
| OCR | Tesseract (`pytesseract`) |
| Vision fallback | Qwen2.5-VL via LM Studio |
| PDF generation | `markdown` + `xhtml2pdf` (pure Python, no system binaries required) |
| Frontend | React (Vite), Axios |

---

## Getting started

### Backend

```bash
cd backend
python -m venv venv
venv\Scripts\activate        # Windows
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and set at minimum:

```
OLLAMA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434
OLLAMA_API_KEY=                 # required if using a cloud-hosted Ollama endpoint
QDRANT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:6333
```

Run database migrations:

```bash
alembic upgrade head
```

> **If you hit `no such table` or `duplicate column` errors on migrate**, your
> `alembic_version` tracking table is probably out of sync with the actual
> schema (this can happen if tables were ever created via `create_all()`
> outside of Alembic). Reset with:
> ```bash
> python -c "
> import asyncio
> from sqlalchemy import text
> from app.db.session import engine
> async def reset():
>     async with engine.begin() as conn:
>         await conn.execute(text('DROP TABLE IF EXISTS alembic_version'))
> asyncio.run(reset())
> "
> alembic stamp base
> alembic upgrade head
> ```
> The migrations in this repo are written to be idempotent (they check for
> existing columns/tables before creating them), so this is safe to re-run.

Start Qdrant (Docker is easiest):

```bash
docker run -p 6333:6333 qdrant/qdrant
```

Pull the required Ollama models:

```bash
ollama pull qwen2.5:latest
ollama pull nomic-embed-text:latest
ollama pull llama3.1
```

Run the API:

```bash
uvicorn app.main:app --reload
```

### Frontend

```bash
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev
```

---

## Configuration notes

All settings live in `app/core/config.py` (Pydantic `Settings`, overridable via
`.env`). A few worth understanding:

- **`LLM_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS`** — this is an *application-level* budget used to
  decide how much context gets packed into a prompt. It only matters if the
  LLM provider actually passes it to Ollama as `num_ctx`; otherwise Ollama
  silently applies its own default (2048 tokens for most models) regardless
  of how big a prompt you send. Check `app/chat/llm_providers/ollama_provider.py`
  to confirm `num_ctx=settings.LLM_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS` is actually being
  passed to `ChatOllama` before assuming a larger config value does anything.
- **`OLLAMA_API_KEY`** — used to authenticate to a cloud-hosted Ollama
  endpoint via a `Bearer` header. Several places in this codebase have
  historically had a bug where the *URL* was sent as the bearer token instead
  of the API key — if `/health/ready`'s `llm_provider` check or chat requests
  are failing auth against a cloud endpoint, check for `Bearer {OLLAMA_URL}`
  instead of `Bearer {OLLAMA_API_KEY}` in the relevant provider file(s).
- **Hardware sizing for local Ollama**: a 7B model at Q4 quantization plus an
  embedding model plus a cross-encoder reranker will contend for the same
  GPU/RAM. On 8GB VRAM / 16GB RAM, `num_ctx=8192` is a reasonable ceiling;
  pushing to 16K+ context risks OOM or silent CPU fallback (slow).

---

## Known architectural decisions worth knowing before you touch these areas

**PDF table extraction** (`app/parsing/pdf_parser.py`) uses PyMuPDF's
`page.find_tables()` on digital (non-scanned) pages only. Scanned/OCR/vision
pages do not currently get table structure extracted — this is a documented
limitation (surfaced in `ParsedDocument.warnings`), not a silent gap.

**Financial fact extraction** (`app/extraction/fact_extractor.py`) is
table-row-driven when `doc.tables` is populated (matches row labels like
"total revenue", "net income" etc. against the adjacent numeric cell), and
falls back to regex-over-raw-text only when no tables are available (e.g. a
scanned financial document). Table-driven facts get higher confidence
(0.85) than raw-text regex fallback (0.5).

**Chunking is type-routed** (`app/chunking/registry.py`). Financial
statement-family documents (`financial_statement`, `bank_statement`,
`tax_document`, `audit_report`) use `FinancialStatementChunker`, which
delegates tables to `TableChunker` (row-batched, header-preserved) and
handles narrative sections (MD&A, notes, auditor's report) via
token-windowed chunking bounded by `max_tokens` — deliberately *not* using
`InvoiceChunker`'s "one unbounded chunk per section" pattern, since narrative
financial-statement sections can be arbitrarily long.

**Filename-scoped retrieval**: when a chat question names a specific document
(either by typed filename or, preferably, by an explicit document picker in
the UI), the system resolves that to a `document_id` via
`DocumentRepository.get_by_filename()` (or the picker's own selection) rather
than relying on Qdrant payload filtering by filename string. If a named
document can't be resolved, retrieval returns no results and surfaces that
explicitly rather than silently falling back to an unfiltered, whole-corpus
search — this is intentional, to avoid answering from the wrong document.

**Document-scoped chat memory**: when a chat conversation is scoped to
specific document(s) (via `Conversation.active_document_ids`), the assistant
maintains a short LLM-summarized scratchpad (`Conversation.scratchpad_notes`,
capped ~150 words) across turns in that conversation. This is a lighter,
session-only counterpart to the Audit Agent's persistent cross-document
memory — it resets when the conversation's document scope changes and does
not use the audit agent's compaction/severity-ranking machinery.

**Audit Agent memory** (`app/audit_agent/memory.py`) hard-caps findings
independently of the LLM's own compaction: critical/high risk flags are never
dropped regardless of memory size; lower-priority findings and open questions
are trimmed first. Compaction triggers above `AUDIT_MEMORY_TOKEN_LIMIT`.

---

## Known gaps / things to fix before production

- [ ] `app/models/document.py` has no tenant/org-scoping field — only
      `user_id`. Any multi-user-per-org sharing story needs this added before
      filename-resolution or document-picker features can be trusted across
      users in the same organization.
- [ ] `alembic/versions/e773abb84b4a_*` ("baseline") is actually a duplicate
      of the audit-tables migration, not a real schema baseline — the real
      baseline (users, documents, conversations, etc.) has never been
      captured as a migration; those tables exist only via `create_all()`.
      Worth generating a proper baseline revision at some point via
      `alembic stamp` + a full `--autogenerate` pass against a clean schema.
- [ ] `GenericChunker`'s overlap logic carries forward exactly one prior
      block regardless of size, rather than a token-bounded amount — imprecise
      but not incorrect. `FinancialStatementChunker`'s narrative-section
      handling currently inherits the same behavior.
- [ ] `estimate_tokens()` (`app/chunking/token_utils.py`) is `len(text) // 4`
      — a rough approximation, not a real tokenizer. Fine for chunking
      heuristics; don't rely on it for exact budget enforcement.
- [ ] Regenerating a chat message currently re-derives context from scratch
      rather than reusing exactly what the original message was answered
      against in all cases — verify this matches your expectations for
      document-scoped conversations.

---

## Repository layout (backend)

```
app/
  api/v1/            FastAPI routers (one file per resource)
  audit_agent/        Audit run prompts, batching, memory management
  chat/                Prompt building, citation extraction, response validation,
                        token budgeting, document-scoped context loading, LLM providers
  chunking/            Type-routed chunkers + registry
  classification/      Rule-based + AI-based document type classification
  canonical/           Unified CanonicalDocument schema
  core/                Settings, security, logging, exceptions
  db/                  Session/engine setup
  embeddings/          Embedding provider abstraction
  extraction/          Fact/entity/line-item extraction
  models/              SQLAlchemy ORM models
  parsing/             Format-specific parsers (PDF, OCR, vision fallback)
  reranking/           Cross-encoder reranking + diversity/dedup logic
  repositories/        DB access layer, one per aggregate
  retrieval/           Query processing, filter extraction, BM25, fusion
  rule_engine/         Deterministic risk-finding rules
  schemas/             Pydantic request/response models
  services/            Business logic orchestration, one per feature area
  vectorstore/         Qdrant provider + payload building
migrations/            Alembic revisions
frontend/
  src/api/             Axios API client functions, one file per resource
  src/pages/           Top-level page components (Chat, Audit, Documents, ...)
  src/components/      Shared UI components
```

